  "plain_english": "`pilot_V1F_ARGUMENT_MAP.md`. Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**The self is the Lord: against the Buddhist no-self**\n\nThe upoddhāta, the preliminary of the whole work, must first clear the ground — by showing who denies\nthe self's lordship and why their denial fails. Not everyone is a real opponent. The materialist, the\nCārvāka, cannot even argue: having no means of proof of their own, they cannot refute the other side\nwithout first adopting the very conventions of proof they reject. The Sāṃkhya, with its many selves or\nits one self with hidden lordship, is not wholly far from us. The real opponent — the one who must be\nmet head-on — is the **Buddhist**, the Sautrāntika, who denies the self outright. It is his no-self that\nthe eleven verses of this vimarśa set out and answer.\n\nThe Buddhist's attack is twofold. First, he denies the self that we claim to perceive: \"the self is\nnot seen,\" he says, \"only the momentary cognitions flash by, with their own-mark appearances.\" Second,\nhe denies the self that we might infer: memory and the synthesis, he holds, are otherwise established —\nthey are only the work of the impressions in the stream, with no abiding owner behind them. So the\nself, whether taken as perceived or as inferred, is rejected; and with it, he concludes, the whole\napparatus of the knowledge-action relation — the very lordship we ascribe to the self — collapses, for\nthere is no one to be the knower and the doer.\n\nAbhinavagupta meets him on both fronts. To the denial of the perceived self, he answers with the\n\"I\"-cognition itself (ahaṃpratyaya): the self is not some object hidden behind the perceptions — it is\npresent, unmistakably, in the very act of saying \"I.\" The \"I\" is not a thing seen among others; it is\nthe seeing itself, that which is present in every cognition, never itself absent. The ādi — the stream\nof the senses, the breath, the knowledge — all presuppose this \"I\" that runs through them. The Buddhist's\n\"the self is not seen\" fails: the self is the seer, and the seer is never not seen, for he is the\nseeing.\n\nAnd the memory and the synthesis, far from being otherwise established, themselves require the abiding\nself. A memory reaches across the break of experience and says \"I saw this before\" — and only an\nabiding self could hold the before and the after together in the single \"I.\" The stream of moments\ncannot do this; moments have no thread by which to re-collect. So memory and the synthesis, which the\nBuddhist thought freed him from the self, in truth demand the self. And the knowledge-action relation —\nthe knowing and the doing — is precisely this self's lordship: the one who knows and the one who acts\nis the self, and that is what it is to be the Lord.\n\nSo the upoddhāta's defense is complete in outline. The Cārvāka cannot argue, the Sāṃkhya is near; the\nreal opponent, the Buddhist no-self, is met and answered. The self is established even by the\n\"I\"-cognition; memory and the synthesis require the abiding self; and the knowledge-action relation —\nthe knowing and the doing of the one who says \"I\" — is the self's lordship. The self is real, and the\nself is the Lord.\n\n- **Preserved:** the Cārvāka cannot argue (no means of proof); the Sāṃkhya not wholly far; the\n  Sautrāntika as the pūrvapakṣin; the 11-verse plan (reject the perceived self by dṛśyānupalabdhi;\n  reject the inferred self by the memory/synthesis otherwise-established; the qualities→holder; the\n  jñāna-svarūpa as un-established; the action-relation nowhere established); the siddhāntin's view\n  echoed; the \"ātmaiva tu\" (the self alone); the 'tu' showing the refutation; the ahaṃpratyaya (the\n  \"I\"-cognition establishing the self); the ādi (sense/breath/knowledge stream); the 'nanu' as the\n  objection.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"the self is the seer, and the seer is never not seen, for he is\n  the seeing\" (restates the ahaṃpratyaya); \"moments have no thread by which to re-collect\" (glosses\n  the memory-requires-self point). Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the exact dṛśyānupalabdhi mechanics; the 11-verse precise mapping. Logged, not\n  invented.",
  "sanskrit_cite": "śrīmahāmāheśvaraśrīmadācāryābhinavaguptaviracitāyāmīśvarapratyabhijñ | āvivṛtivimarśinyāmupoddhātavimarśaḥ | prathamaḥ | || | 1 | || | atha | dvitīyo | vimarśaḥ | | | ānandamamṛtapūrṇaṃ | sāmanase",
